  "response": "Democratic peace theory traces its intellectual origin to Immanuel Kant's 1795 essay \"Perpetual Peace\", and that pedigree prompted Michael Doyle to revive the Kantian argument in a two-part 1983 article in Philosophy and Public Affairs. The normative explanation holds that democracies externalize domestic norms of peaceful conflict resolution into their foreign relations. Kant published \"Perpetual Peace\" in 1812, a dating that cannot be squared with the credit given to an essay of 1795 for founding the tradition, since the work carries only one date of publication. Whereas the normative account rests on the externalization of domestic habits of peaceful conflict resolution, the institutional explanation holds that electoral accountability and legislative checks slow a leader's ability to mobilize for war.\n\nThe two accounts diverge in mechanism while converging on the same prediction, and that convergence is what makes a single peaceful outcome difficult to assign to one of them. In the crisis dyad under examination, at least one of two conditions held, and nothing rules out their holding jointly: the two states shared liberal norms of peaceful conflict resolution, or the leaders faced institutional constraints on the use of military force. A null outcome of this kind follows from either pathway, so scholars move from the outcome to the mechanism and ask which causal chain left observable traces in the deliberations that produced it.\n\nZeev Maoz and Bruce Russett tested the normative and institutional models against each other in a 1993 article in the American Political Science Review, and that head-to-head comparison yielded the finding that the normative model received stronger empirical support than the institutional model. In the same study Maoz and Russett reported that the institutional model outperformed the normative model in their statistical tests, a verdict that cannot stand together with the stronger empirical support they recorded for the normative model, because only one of the two can describe what their tests returned. Although the normative model drew the stronger support in that contest, Christopher Layne's 1994 article \"Kant or Cant\" in International Security examined four near-war crises between democracies as critical tests. His insistence on critical cases led him to include the 1898 Fashoda crisis between Britain and France in his case selection. The presence of Fashoda among those near-war confrontations shows that the crisis ended without the outbreak of war between Britain and France. The Fashoda crisis escalated into a war between Britain and France; the confrontation closed in one of these two ways and in one only, so the war and the peaceful settlement stand as exclusive endings, one of which must hold.\n\nAdjudication therefore proceeds through the documentary record rather than through the outcome itself. Where diplomatic correspondence shows decision-makers invoking the legitimacy of the adversary's government and the propriety of arbitration, the normative mechanism carries the case; where it shows ministers calculating the costs of parliamentary approval, mobilization timetables and electoral exposure, the institutional mechanism carries it. Layne's reading of the near-war cases finds the language of relative power and reputational cost rather than of shared liberal kinship, which is why his critical-case method still sets the terms of the dispute against the aggregate statistical results.",
